@tomasstrba @ayoy The `notifyDelegate()` was being called before the select (this was before I added any change to the recently closed functionality).

Which seemed to be a bug, given the `notifyDelegate` was the following:

```swift
func notifyDelegate() {
    if index.isUnpinnedTab {
        let newSelectionIndex = self.selectionIndex?.isUnpinnedTab == true ? self.selectionIndex?.item : nil
        delegate?.tabCollectionViewModel(self, didRemoveTabAt: index.item, andSelectTabAt: newSelectionIndex)
    }
}
```

Where the index is the index of the removed tab, and the `newSelectionIndex` is the index of the newly selected tab that is calculated with the `selectionIndex`.

The problem is `select(at: newSelectionIndex, forceChange: forced)` happened **after** the notify delegate. The select method is responsible for changing the value of `selectionIndex`, which made the new selection index in the delegate to be wrong.

I assume it was this way because before, we mainly selected the next or previous tab or, in some cases, the parent tab.

